plan Persistence
title   Persistence
status  archived
goal
    Capture and restore explicitly durable Runtime data through an optional provider, without
    replaying executable work. Keep captures context-closed and storage writes ordered.
    This proposed Plan is pending approval. It establishes no shipped implementation claim.
outcomes
    O1  Optional inert providers preserve typed storage and pure codec boundaries
        Persistence is an optional RuntimeSetup provider with required storage and scope, optional
        synchronous pure codec, and optional identity-only filter. No provider means no persistence
        I/O. RuntimeSetup.construct remains synchronous and inert under
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O3. It retains configuration but acquires no Implementation,
        creates/registers no actor, starts no work, and exposes no handle.

        The exact provider notation and value boundaries appear below. Web Storage and IndexedDB
        adapters normalize typed read/write/remove failures, cancellation, and lifetime. The default
        codec rejects unsupported values without stringification or silent loss. The application
        codec validates opaque domain values synchronously and purely; Flow validates envelopes,
        identities, persistenceVersion, plan compatibility, bounds, and executable-work rejection.
        The shared structural codec and byte bounds belong to @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O2.

        Actor refs and resource/transaction/stream declarations opt in separately with persist true.
        Every default is false. Durable operations never make their actor durable implicitly. Filter
        receives only kind, stable identity, owning stable actor, and canonical K. It may exclude
        declared data; it cannot select undeclared data or inspect P, mutable actor state, services,
        operation results, or storage ownership. Fixtures and story.machine inherit no
        browser/session storage by default.

        The provider is one logical record attached to one Runtime and scope. It is not an
        Implementation, AppPlan input, second Runtime, cross-tab synchronizer, conflict resolver, or
        concurrent same-scope writer protocol. Cross-tab/concurrent writer coordination remains
        unspecified. The codec never performs storage I/O, runs Effects, creates actors, resumes
        work, or decodes a complete boot payload.

        PersistenceStorage has exactly these typed Effect methods:

        | Method | Exact signature |
        | --- | --- |
        | read | (): Effect.Effect<Uint8Array \| undefined, PersistenceStorageError> |
        | write | (value: Uint8Array): Effect.Effect<void, PersistenceStorageError> |
        | remove | (): Effect.Effect<void, PersistenceStorageError> |

        Persistence has exactly these fields:

        | Field | Exact type |
        | --- | --- |
        | storage | PersistenceStorage |
        | scope | string |
        | codec | PersistenceCodec |
        | filter? | (entry: PersistenceEntry) => boolean |

        PersistenceCodec has exactly these synchronous methods:

        | Method | Exact signature |
        | --- | --- |
        | encode | (value: unknown, slot: PersistenceSlot): PersistenceValue |
        | decode | (value: PersistenceValue, slot: PersistenceSlot): unknown |

        The public constructors have these exact call forms:

        | Function | Exact call form |
        | --- | --- |
        | webStorage | webStorage(storage: Storage): PersistenceStorage |
        | indexedDbStorage | indexedDbStorage(storage: IndexedDBStorage): PersistenceStorage |
        | persistence | persistence(options): Persistence |

        persistence options require storage:PersistenceStorage and scope:string. Optional fields are
        codec?:PersistenceCodec and filter?:(entry:PersistenceEntry)=>boolean. There are no
        additional option fields or overloads.

        PersistenceValue is bounded canonical JSON. PersistenceSlot identifies actor, resource,
        transaction, or stream value slots. PersistenceEntry exposes kind, stable identity, owning
        stable actor, and canonical K only. It exposes no executable P, mutable actor state,
        services, operation results, or storage ownership. These semantic boundaries establish no
        additional public member spelling for Slot, Entry, StorageError, or IndexedDBStorage.

        The [persistence examples](references/README.md) illustrate setup and opt-in.

        Internal storage, codec, identity/version, malformed-data, concurrent-capture, and
        non-durable provider failures retain canonical Diagnostic ownership. FlowPersistenceError is
        only a terminal host compatibility Error where that existing JavaScript name is required. It
        has no specified extra fields or stable message. Raw Cause is retained only at
        readiness/disposal boundaries and never in persisted carriers.
    O2  Durable selection and app-compatible boot preserve exact ownership
        Restore validates against the receiving compiled AppPlan. Preserve app ID, application-owned
        persistenceVersion, durable machine/descriptor/state/event/actor identities, and exact refs.
        No generic identity migration, assertion-cast input, alias, or public mutable hydration is
        supported. Canonical P/K identity follows @plan.Resources.O1. K never reconstructs P,
        supplies capabilities, or authorizes execution. Flow structural validation and application
        value-codec validation remain distinct.

        Follow the readiness order in @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O3:

        1. Validate provider bytes and prepare detached decoded data without actors/store install.
        2. Acquire Implementation services.
        3. Install prepared actor state, operation facts, and one canonical resource store.
        4. Complete initial ensureActor calls and resolve exact context refs.
        5. Seal the graph, establish context baselines, activate, and expose ready handles.

        Failure or incomplete bootstrap exposes no actor, external work, or handle. Restore
        materialized state/memory without invoking fresh-memory initialization or replaying input.
        Retain committed bases, generation fences, overlay provenance, stale-completion facts, actor
        observed-store revisions, exact context bindings, and observed provider revisions. Context
        baselines derive from restored providers, not serialized selected-value overrides.

        Durable actors require authored stable machine-branded actorRef(machine,id,{persist:true}).
        ensureActor owns restore-or-create; createActor remains ID-free, fresh, opaque, and local.
        Ref/lease/registration ownership belongs to @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O4. Stable wire
        identity and length-prefixed UTF-8 segments follow that owner, never Buffer or UTF-16
        length. A restored stable actor remains Runtime-owned and captureable without repeated
        ensureActor.

        Capture registered, non-disposed durable stable actors, including suspended ones, and their
        exact transitive context-provider closure. Membership comes from declaration metadata,
        registration, and fixed bindings. Exclude local/opaque refs, tombstones, disposed actors,
        current-projection membership, and automatic/dynamic/child/subordinate actors. Operation
        durability never changes actor selection.

        Actor facts retain exact ref/machine, state, memory, durable bindings, operation facts,
        issues, referenced refs, and observed revisions. Private durable facts may retain finite
        cursors, timer due/consumed activation facts, and pending mapped outcomes. They never retain
        queue cells, mailboxes, commands, fibers, Scopes, callbacks, services, cursor sessions,
        transports, buffered emissions, executable P, or executable work. Resource data remains one
        canonical base rather than actor-private copies.
    O3  Capture observes one consistent store cut and a closed context graph
        Capture selects stable actor leases, observes the canonical store once, and atomically reads
        published actor state. Each observedStoreRevision must be no newer than captured revision.
        Capture occurs before or after a completed actor/store publication, never through its
        middle. Unrelated state-only actor turns may be captured at different instants. This
        consistency rule creates no public transaction/barrier API or simultaneous graph-wide
        publication. Shared publication and causal-completeness laws belong to
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6.

        The selected durable closure must have completed context propagation. Preserve each exact
        provider ref and revision observed by its consumer. Reject mixed store cuts, newer actor
        revisions, concurrent closure changes, orphan or partial payloads, duplicated canonical
        bases, and selected-context overrides. A suspended durable actor is eligible when its
        context cut is complete. Suspended context debt causes retryable capture conflict until
        resume/recovery completes propagation; never silently omit it or serialize a stale cut.
        Capture performs no external work.

        Required direct/transitive opaque, nonpersistent, or filter-excluded providers make the
        entire selected consumer closure fail NonDurableContextProvider. Never promote, serialize,
        recreate, substitute, rebind, or silently omit a required provider or consumer. This is
        terminal for that selection. Concurrent mismatch instead reports the canonical Diagnostic
        code ConcurrentCapture and is retryable. Exact codes and classification belong to
        @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O1. No runtime alias or second error family is introduced.

        NonDurableContextProvider identifies the durable consumer ID, opaque provider diagnostic
        ID, provider machine ID, and every failing contextBindings.<key> path. Preserve these facts
        for each invalid direct or transitive binding through canonical Diagnostic paths and scalar
        details. Multiple invalid bindings cannot hide one another. No alternate diagnostic shape
        or array-valued details are introduced.

        Remediation names actorRef(providerMachine,id) and runtime.ensureActor(ref,...).
        Never suggest ID-bearing runtime.createActor, provider substitution, or rebinding.
    O4  Hydration normalizes operations without replay
        Install one canonical resource store, rebuild resource/transaction projections from exact
        refs and store truth, and restore context providers before consumers in dependency order.
        Evaluate selectors and install derived context as a silent baseline. Do not manufacture
        onContext events or restore a second selected-context authority. Drain pending mapped
        outcomes before rematerializing continuing declarations. Install this baseline before
        continuing-activity reconciliation or handle escape under @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O3.
        Capture/baseline installation performs no external work.

        In-flight resource execution never resumes. Key-only entries stay passive until a live
        binding supplies complete P. Equal-key running generations retain pinned P; automatic
        generations use the oldest eligible live binding, while refetch uses caller P. Restored
        collection follows descriptor policy under @plan.Resources.O3, without invented lookup,
        gcTime, or retention defaults. Actor-effective reads add only that owner's previews under
        @plan.Transactions.O3. Generation/value lifetimes may restart only where @plan.Resources.O3
        permits; actor/global counters never reset.

        Running streams retain latest/hasValue/count/generation/declaration/ownership projection.
        After pending outcomes drain, executable live P starts a fresh owned generation without
        emission replay under @plan.Streams.O3. Terminal streams never restart; missing P fails
        closed with precise diagnostics. K never reconstructs P or a transport session.

        Restore pending remote transactions as unknown/reconcileRequired unless evidence rules out
        adapter invocation under @plan.Transactions.O4. A saved not-yet-invoked fact describes only
        its captured cut. Delayed or coalesced writes cannot prove no invocation occurred afterward.
        Restore pending local transaction occurrences as interrupted without replay.
        Reconciliation uses the same adapter-recorded identity; Runtime restore does not manage the
        independent adapter journal. Terminal transactions restore only in their accepted state
        representation. Never replay finite lookup/commit work, input, initialization, or old
        emissions. Persisted counters and generations are non-negative safe integers with reserved
        terminal-publication and Runtime-cleanup headroom; exhausted restoration fails before
        installation rather than wrapping, reusing identity, or partially publishing.
    O5  Runtime-owned observation and ordered writes preserve the last good record
        After successful restore/bootstrap, Runtime observes committed publications through the
        consistent-cut boundary in @O3. Observation is distinct from inspection sinks and export.
        Runtime serializes writes per scope and may coalesce accepted publications. Older writes
        cannot replace a newer accepted stored state. No actor owns the persistence provider.
        These writes do not replace the independent adapter-journal acknowledgment in
        @plan.Transactions.O4. No-provider and nonpersistent actors remain supported there.
        Persisted transaction facts are Flow's permitted attempt/invocation evidence at capture.
        A last-good record can predate later adapter invocation or dispatch; @O4 preserves that
        uncertainty at restore. @plan.Transactions.O4 excludes capture or recovery of the adapter's
        private journal and identity. Application-owned reconciliation remains outside Runtime
        restore.

        Write failure preserves committed Runtime truth and the last successful stored record.
        Report the provider's write failure; never fail an actor turn after its committed
        publication. Codec failure commits no Runtime mutation. Disposal stops observation,
        finalizes the latest accepted write, then releases provider ownership. Cancellation and
        cleanup remain typed and preserve honest failure evidence under
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5 and @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2.

        StorageFailure, CodecFailure, IdentityVersionMismatch, MalformedData, ConcurrentCapture, and
        NonDurableContextProvider retain canonical Diagnostic ownership. Structural/bound/
        decompression/app-domain/cleanup/I/O/invariant failures remain distinguishable under
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O2 and @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O1. No raw parser/library
        exception or generic corruption/undefined result escapes. FlowPersistenceError remains only
        the terminal host compatibility envelope described under @O1. Raw Cause is retained only at
        readiness/disposal boundaries, never in persisted carriers.
decisions
    D1  Durability is explicit declaration ownership
        Separate actor and operation opt-ins plus an exclusion-only filter determine selection.
        Context closure cannot silently weaken that selection. Captures and restore retain data and
        identity, never authority to replay external work.
    D2  Restore and writes belong to one Runtime lifecycle
        Validate detached data before service acquisition/install. Publish no partial bootstrap.
        Serialize writes and preserve the last good record independently of actor turn success.
constraints
    C1  Keep provider and codec authority bounded
        Add no cross-tab synchronization, same-scope writer protocol, conflict resolution, public
        boot decoder/payload, mutable hydration API, generic migration/fingerprint API, or automatic
        durability. Share artifacts are not persistence or boot inputs under
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O5. No provider I/O occurs during synchronous construction.
    C2  Keep exact identity and non-executable restore
        Add no automatic/dynamic/child/subordinate actor aliases, actor-private canonical cache,
        serialized selected-context truth, implicit finite retry, terminal-stream restart, or
        key-to-input inversion. All structural bytes use @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O2 bounds.
verification
    V1  Provider API, codec purity, and declaration defaults
        Establish @O1. Planned, unbuilt checks:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/persistence/test/provider-types.test.ts` and
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/persistence/test/provider.test.ts`. Compile exact
        read/write/remove Effects, required scope, optional pure codec/filter, and host adapter
        forms. Test absent/present provider construction and default-off declarations. Pass with
        zero construction I/O/work/actor creation and no storage inheritance by fixtures or machine
        Stories. Filters must observe only allowed identity metadata and never opt in undeclared
        data. Unsupported/default/custom codec values must fail without lossy coercion.
    V2  Detached boot, exact actors, and durable selection
        Establish @O2. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/persistence/test/bootstrap.test.ts`. Restore
        compatible/incompatible app/version/identity records and inject failure at each ordered
        readiness boundary. Pass with detached validation before Implementation acquisition, one
        canonical store, exact stable refs, provider-before-consumer silent baseline, and no
        fresh-memory/input replay. Failed bootstrap must expose no handle/actor/external work and
        must release acquired ownership. A restored stable actor must remain captureable without
        another ensureActor call. Exclude local/disposed/tombstone/undeclared entries and executable
        runtime values. Preserve all specified private identity/cursor/observed-revision facts.
    V3  Capture closure and race proofs
        Establish @O3. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/persistence/test/capture.test.ts`. Race actor/store commit,
        capture, provider propagation, registration, and disposal. Pass with one store revision,
        atomic actor reads, exact binding refs/revisions, and no half cut, orphan, partial payload,
        or actor revision newer than captured store. Unrelated state-only turns may differ. Test
        complete suspended actors and suspended context debt; debt must yield retryable
        ConcurrentCapture until resume/recovery completes propagation.

        Select consumers with opaque, persist-false, filter-excluded, direct, and transitive
        providers. Pass with whole-closure NonDurableContextProvider and no promotion/substitution/
        omission. Concurrent closure mismatch must be retryable ConcurrentCapture. Capture itself
        must invoke no external adapter, emit no context event, and mutate no Runtime truth.

        Reject a selection with two invalid context slots, including one transitive opaque
        provider. Pass when diagnostics preserve each durable consumer ID, opaque provider
        diagnostic ID, provider machine ID, and every failing contextBindings.<key> path.
        Remediation must name actorRef and ensureActor; reject suggestions to create an ID-bearing
        local actor, substitute a provider, or rebind an existing consumer.
    V4  No-replay hydration and counter headroom
        Establish @O4. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/persistence/test/hydration.test.ts`. Hydrate key-only
        resources, competing eligible live P, previews, pending local/remote transactions, and
        zero/value/running/terminal streams. Pass with passive key-only entries, deterministic P
        choice, owner-only previews, local pending transactions restored as interrupted without
        replay, remote pending transactions restored as unknown/reconcileRequired unless evidence
        rules out adapter invocation, and fresh stream generation after pending outcomes drain.
        For remote work, an interrupted restore requires evidence excluding invocation after
        capture. No finite work, input, initialization,
        context event, or old emission may replay. Missing P fails closed; terminal streams never
        start. Validate every counter/terminal/cleanup reserve before installation; exhausted input
        changes no actor/store/ownership and starts no work.

        Exercise a delayed-write crash across @O4 and @O5:

        1. Save a queued pending occurrence before adapter invocation.
        2. Let its adapter acknowledge its independent journal and dispatch remotely.
        3. Suppress the newer Runtime write, crash, and restore exactly the earlier saved bytes.
        4. Require unknown/reconcileRequired, preserved Flow attempt evidence, and no replay or new
           request. Capture no adapter-private identity and recover no private journal.

        Separately prove any remote transaction restored as interrupted has evidence excluding
        invocation after the saved cut. Absence of a saved invocation fact is insufficient.
    V5  Ordered writes, failure retention, and disposal
        Establish @O5. Planned, unbuilt check:
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/persistence/test/writes.test.ts`. Exercise synchronous Web
        Storage and asynchronous IndexedDB read/write/remove failures, delayed older writes,
        coalescing, codec failure, cancellation, and disposal with accepted writes pending. Pass
        when the latest accepted write is finalized before provider release, an older completion
        never replaces newer truth, and failures preserve Runtime state and last good record. A
        published actor turn must remain successful despite later write failure. Combine delayed
        writes with the post-capture dispatch/crash scenario in @V4; last-good-record retention must
        not imply interrupted remote truth. Verify owned diagnostic code/classification/path/bound
        and Cause retention at permitted boundaries
        through @plan.DiagnosticsContract.V2 and @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.V2.
